">Pickens County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/pierce-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Pierce County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/pike-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Pike County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/polk-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Polk County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/putnam-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Putnam County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/rabun-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Rabun County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/richmond-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Richmond County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/rockdale-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Rockdale County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/screven-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Screven County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/spalding-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Spalding County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/stephens-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Stephens County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/sumter-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Sumter County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/talbot-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Talbot County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/tattnall-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Tattnall County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/terrell-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Terrell County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/thomas-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Thomas County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/tift-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Tift County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/toombs-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Toombs County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/towns-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Towns County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/troup-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Troup County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/twiggs-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Twiggs County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/union-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Union County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/upson-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Upson County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/walker-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Walker County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/walton-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Walton County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/ware-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Ware County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/warren-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Warren County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/wayne-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Wayne County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/white-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">White County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/whitfield-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Whitfield County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/wilkinson-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Wilkinson County, GA Property Tax Guide</a> <a href=\"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/worth-county-ga-property-tax-guide/\">Worth County, GA Property Tax Guide</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"chb-cta\" style=\"margin:32px 0;border-radius:10px;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#0F1C2E 0%,#16293F 100%);padding:28px 32px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;color:#fff;\"><div style=\"max-width:600px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;color:#E0A83C;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;\">Selling in Georgia?</div><div style=\"font-weight:800;font-size:20px;line-height:1.35;color:#fff;\">We buy Georgia houses as-is, with no commission, no repairs and no closing costs to you.</div></div><a href=\"#\" onclick=\"var f=document.querySelector('iframe[data-form-id]');if(f){f.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'center'});return false;}\" style=\"flex-shrink:0;padding:14px 24px;border-radius:8px;background:#E0A83C;color:#16203A;font-size:14px;font-weight:800;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;\">Get My Cash Offer &rarr;</a></div></div>\n<!-- /wp:html --<div class='chb-rw'><h2>What should a Georgia home seller know before listing?</h2><p>A Georgia sale touches a county tax commissioner, a board of tax assessors, a clerk of superior court and, for troubled properties, the courts. Which office matters depends on the problem, so this page maps the whole state and the guides on this site.</p>\n<p>Georgia had 11,302,748 residents in mid 2025, up 5.5 percent from the 2020 base, and the 2020 to 2024 survey shows 601,304 veterans and 4,074,366 households in the state. Those households are the owners and renters served by 159 county governments.</p><h2>Which Georgia numbers help a seller judge the local market?</h2><p>Statewide survey figures give a rough backdrop, and the county gives the detail. Census tables show monthly owner costs of $1,783 for households carrying a mortgage and $541 for those without one, and a median household income of $77,353 in 2024 dollars. Building departments issued 61,196 permits during 2025, so new construction competes with resale homes in many places.</p>\n<p>Employers tell a similar story of scale: 261,320 employer establishments and 4,405,730 jobs in 2023. These are averages across 159 very different counties, so a buyer in one corner of the state may face conditions nothing like another. Look up recent sales near your own address at the assessor or clerk's office rather than relying on a state number.</p><h2>Which office handles which Georgia property question?</h2><table>\n<caption>Georgia county offices and the topics they handle, per the Georgia Department of Revenue property tax FAQ and the Clerks' Cooperative Authority</caption>\n<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Where to ask</th><th>Rule stated by the source</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Paying a tax bill, tax liens, intangible recording tax</td><td>County tax commissioner</td><td>One bill covering county, school and state levies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appeal of a value</td><td>County board of tax assessors</td><td>Written appeal on the uniform state form</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Homestead exemption</td><td>Tax commissioner or tax assessor, depending on the county</td><td>Primary residences qualify; some counties add more relief</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Real estate transfer tax</td><td>Clerk of superior court</td><td>Clerk certifies payment first; seller pays by default</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Foreclosure sale</td><td>County courthouse steps</td><td>Public auction at the courthouse once a month</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eviction case</td><td>Magistrate court, usually</td><td>Tenant answers within seven days of service</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><h2>How is Georgia property tax set, and who gets the bill?</h2><p>Each of the 159 counties runs its own digest. The assessors value every parcel as of January 1, and the state formula takes the assessed amount (40 percent of market value), removes the standard $2,000 state homestead exemption, and applies the millage rate adopted where the house sits. No statewide revaluation calendar exists, so one county may revisit values yearly while another changes little.</p>\n<p>The revenue agency says most counties set the due date at December 20, with the bill payable over 60 days from billing. County, school and state tax all arrive on one bill from the tax commissioner. Some counties add homestead relief beyond the state exemption, which is a reason to call the local office before assuming the standard figure applies to your house.</p><h2>What is the route for contesting a Georgia assessed value?</h2><p>Disputes begin in writing at the county assessors, and a late filing forfeits the dispute, so mark the mailing date on the notice the day it arrives. The state revenue agency does not rule on individual parcels, which is why a form mailed there goes nowhere.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A Board of Equalization hearing is the usual path for a homeowner</li>\n<li>A hearing officer is reserved for costly non-homestead property</li>\n<li>Arbitration needs an appraisal from a certified appraiser, and fee responsibility follows whose number is closer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After any of those, superior court is the last stop. Our county guides give the local assessor contact for the first filing.</p><h2>What happens to property tax when the house sells?</h2><p>Ownership on January 1 fixes who owes that year's ad valorem tax, and the revenue agency states that a sale on January 2 still leaves the January 1 owner liable for the full year, with no refund for a partial year. A contract can split the bill between buyer and seller, so write the split down and request a payoff figure from the tax commissioner before closing.</p>\n<p>A separate excise tax applies at the deed. The Clerks' Cooperative Authority prints it as a dollar for the first thousand of price plus ten cents for every further hundred or part of a hundred, and the seller carries it unless the contract assigns it elsewhere. No deed can be recorded until the clerk certifies payment. The revenue agency says Georgia treats capital gains the same as other income, so ask the Taxpayer Services Division how a home sale gain is reported.</p><h2>What do Georgia foreclosure and eviction rules mean for sellers?</h2><p>Lenders in Georgia auction homes without a lawsuit. The borrower gets a certified or overnight letter no later than a month before the sale, and the auction runs on the first Tuesday at the county courthouse. Atlanta Legal Aid lists a pay-off sale ahead of that date as one way out, which makes timing the central issue for an owner in default. Our foreclosure guide goes through each step.</p>\n<p>Landlords go through the magistrate courts rather than changing locks. Tenants answer court papers within a week of service, and a seller handing a rented house to a buyer must pass the tenant's deposit along or refund it. The eviction notice and eviction process guides on this site cover those steps one at a time.</p><h2>Where can Georgia owners and tenants find free legal help?</h2><p>Two nonprofit programs run the GeorgiaLegalAid.org site, Atlanta Legal Aid Society and the Georgia Legal Services Program, and the site carries plain-language guides on foreclosure and landlord and tenant problems. Atlanta Legal Aid keeps offices in Fulton, DeKalb, Cobb, South Fulton and Clayton, and Gwinnett, and the Attorney General's page sends residents of every other county to the Georgia Legal Services Program.</p>\n<p>The Georgia Courts website also hosts a landlord and tenant self-help page, with links to magistrate court forms. Owners facing a sale date or tenants served with papers should contact one of these programs early, because the answer deadline after service is only seven days.</p><h2>Where do the city and county guides fit in?</h2><p>Think of the guides in three layers. State pages carry rules that hold everywhere, such as the assessment ratio, the appeal window and the foreclosure calendar. County pages cover the offices that bill, value and record, and city pages describe the place a house is actually sold in. Probate, tax sale, estate, landlord and tenant topics sit with the state layer because the law is statewide.</p>\n<p>When a local detail is missing, the tax commissioner, the tax assessor and the clerk of superior court are the three offices that can answer it. Bring the parcel number or the street address, and ask for the current bill, the current assessed value and any recorded liens in writing.</p></div><h2>More questions about Georgia Guides</h2><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What is the assessment ratio for Georgia homes?</h3><p>Forty percent of fair market value. Tax is figured on that assessed amount after the homestead exemption is subtracted, then multiplied by the local millage rate.</p><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When are Georgia property taxes due?</h3><p>Normally December 20 in most counties, though some counties set a different date. Owners have 60 days from the date of billing to pay, so check the bill from your county tax commissioner for the exact deadline.</p><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who pays the Georgia real estate transfer tax?</h3><p>The seller, by default. A sales contract can shift the cost to the buyer, and the clerk of superior court collects it before the deed goes on record.</p><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What is the deadline to contest a Georgia assessment notice?</h3><p>Forty-five days from the mailing date on the notice. Send the written appeal to the county assessors rather than the state, and pick one of three paths on the form: Board of Equalization, hearing officer or arbitrator.</p><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can a Georgia lender foreclose without suing?</h3><p>Yes. Under the power of sale in the loan documents, the lender can auction the house outside court once the borrower has had a written notice at least 30 days ahead. A borrower who spots a defect can still ask a judge for relief.</p><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"FAQPage\", \"mainEntity\": [{\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"What is the assessment ratio for Georgia homes?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Forty percent of fair market value. Tax is figured on that assessed amount after the homestead exemption is subtracted, then multiplied by the local millage rate.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"When are Georgia property taxes due?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Normally December 20 in most counties, though some counties set a different date. Owners have 60 days from the date of billing to pay, so check the bill from your county tax commissioner for the exact deadline.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Who pays the Georgia real estate transfer tax?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"The seller, by default. A sales contract can shift the cost to the buyer, and the clerk of superior court collects it before the deed goes on record.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"What is the deadline to contest a Georgia assessment notice?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Forty-five days from the mailing date on the notice. Send the written appeal to the county assessors rather than the state, and pick one of three paths on the form: Board of Equalization, hearing officer or arbitrator.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Can a Georgia lender foreclose without suing?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Yes. Under the power of sale in the loan documents, the lender can auction the house outside court once the borrower has had a written notice at least 30 days ahead. A borrower who spots a defect can still ask a judge for relief.\"}}]}</script>>", "status": "publish", "rank_math_title": "Georgia Guides by City and County | Cash Home Buyers", "rank_math_description": "Every Georgia guide in one place: property tax, probate, foreclosure and selling a house, by statewide topic, city and county. Official sources.", "rank_math_focus_keyword": "georgia property guides"}]